2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}"] ["answer-4220d81b-5fee-5b30-8b21-5a4d1398318f" :answer/id #uuid "4220d81b-5fee-5b30-8b21-5a4d1398318f"] ["answer-4220d81b-5fee-5b30-8b21-5a4d1398318f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-4220d81b-5fee-5b30-8b21-5a4d1398318f" :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}"] ["answer-5962da49-0814-5ce2-838a-0da88eb72219" :answer/id #uuid "5962da49-0814-5ce2-838a-0da88eb72219"] ["answer-5962da49-0814-5ce2-838a-0da88eb72219" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-5962da49-0814-5ce2-838a-0da88eb72219" :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi \\}"] ["answer-895ec483-a133-5e14-a169-801e1f3a0cdf" :answer/id #uuid "895ec483-a133-5e14-a169-801e1f3a0cdf"] ["answer-895ec483-a133-5e14-a169-801e1f3a0cdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-895ec483-a133-5e14-a169-801e1f3a0cdf" :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2\\}"] [17592186281276 :answer-field/correct "answer-e6783016-1b3a-5048-9e49-5135f2d7cdae"] [17592186281276 :answer-field/choices "answer-20ef2694-93c9-5433-b86a-aa9f99a0bde9"] [17592186281276 :answer-field/choices "answer-e6783016-1b3a-5048-9e49-5135f2d7cdae"] [17592186281276 :answer-field/choices "answer-4220d81b-5fee-5b30-8b21-5a4d1398318f"] [17592186281276 :answer-field/choices "answer-5962da49-0814-5ce2-838a-0da88eb72219"] [17592186281276 :answer-field/choices "answer-895ec483-a133-5e14-a169-801e1f3a0cdf"] ["question-1b4d1e3e-36ae-5b22-9e31-1455caee6c30" :question/id #uuid "1b4d1e3e-36ae-5b22-9e31-1455caee6c30"] ["question-1b4d1e3e-36ae-5b22-9e31-1455caee6c30" :question/math-academy-id "q-161852"] ["question-1b4d1e3e-36ae-5b22-9e31-1455caee6c30" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b15c12a1cabbab36feab2d15e1ac3d73e8d8c26afc14a8b820e2ca6f57d7d48d.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2z,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as"] ["question-1b4d1e3e-36ae-5b22-9e31-1455caee6c30" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $(0,0,1).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2z$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2z \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/66/66812de081c1bbfe515162ab628806717b5602ec1a6cc61eaf22514f91e9c0ea.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$"] ["question-1b4d1e3e-36ae-5b22-9e31-1455caee6c30" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-2ef5564a-71c2-51d9-a5c5-1e430a20af03" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef5564a-71c2-51d9-a5c5-1e430a20af0