2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/14/1499d492c526bad77e81aca1128b6160cc574a209ad7706e8d89b3012112e1df.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"] ["answer-ec725f35-2519-51f8-8cc0-4a11c90e6631" :answer/id #uuid "ec725f35-2519-51f8-8cc0-4a11c90e6631"] ["answer-ec725f35-2519-51f8-8cc0-4a11c90e6631" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-ec725f35-2519-51f8-8cc0-4a11c90e6631" :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$"] ["answer-2ca9eaa8-0cf0-5859-b374-fdd5806c8ab5" :answer/id #uuid "2ca9eaa8-0cf0-5859-b374-fdd5806c8ab5"] ["answer-2ca9eaa8-0cf0-5859-b374-fdd5806c8ab5" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-2ca9eaa8-0cf0-5859-b374-fdd5806c8ab5" :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$"] ["answer-a973e4e0-ee5f-5fc6-ab29-b94e84360208" :answer/id #uuid "a973e4e0-ee5f-5fc6-ab29-b94e84360208"] ["answer-a973e4e0-ee5f-5fc6-ab29-b94e84360208" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-a973e4e0-ee5f-5fc6-ab29-b94e84360208" :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$"] ["answer-722d91c6-40fa-56a4-867b-919bace76541" :an