ts one-half of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2865e838521a8864ad76d72e520d4b128a8fceb8c18cb9c35ba10134419c4eb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"] ["answer-0888c7a6-5da3-5a1f-b87d-986ab2864e10" :answer/id #uuid "0888c7a6-5da3-5a1f-b87d-986ab2864e10"] ["answer-0888c7a6-5da3-5a1f-b87d-986ab2864e10" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-0888c7a6-5da3-5a1f-b87d-986ab2864e10" :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$"] ["answer-7f98c60e-4e49-520e-a5f5-776539a57fcc" :answer/id #uuid "7f98c60e-4e49-520e-a5f5-776539a57fcc"] ["answer-7f98c60e-4e49-520e-a5f5-776539a57fcc" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-7f98c60e-4e49-520e-a5f5-776539a57fcc" :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$"] ["answer-18faa7fa-7eb2-59fb-8bb6-a8207578ac26" :answer/id #uuid "18faa7fa-7eb