ector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a6985012edbaf28ca42d88fd47e3769fa53704cad8f8c09021c944d966a3d48.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"] ["answer-5088423b-db3e-54df-b831-a57602e5ebe9" :answer/id #uuid "5088423b-db3e-54df-b831-a57602e5ebe9"] ["answer-5088423b-db3e-54df-b831-a57602e5ebe9" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-5088423b-db3e-54df-b831-a57602e5ebe9" :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}"] ["answer-bfd5bc74-b990-585b-b3cb-9771824e5c92" :answer/id #uuid "bfd5bc74-b990-585b-b3cb-9771824e5c92"] ["answer-bfd5bc74-b990-585b-b3cb-9771824e5c92" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-bfd5bc74-b990-585b-b3cb-9771824e5c92" :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}"] ["answer-6bd62de5-f0c5-582e-9f22-e1174588775b" :answer/id #uuid "6bd62de5-f0c5-582e-9f22-e1174588775b"] ["answer-6bd62de5-f0c5-582e-9f22-e1174588775b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6bd62de5-f0c5-582e-9f22-e1174588775b" :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}"] ["answer-805a0a6c-588a-5652-8708-ef6f81947bad" :answer/id #uuid "805a0a6c-588a-5652-8708-ef6f81947bad"] ["answer-805a0a6c-588a-5652-8708-ef6f81947bad" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-805a0a6c-588a-5652-8708-ef6f81947bad" :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}"] ["answer-f8e6ef4b-e544-53c2-99a8-afb161df11b4" :answer/id #uuid "f8e6ef4b-e544-53c2-99a8-afb161df11b4"] ["answer-f8e6ef4b-e544-53c2-99a8-afb161df11b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f8e6ef4b-e544-53c2-99a8-afb161df11b4" :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}"] [17592186363431 :answer-field/correct "answer-805a0a6c-588a-5652-8708-ef6f81947bad"] [17592186363431 :answer-field/choices "answer-5088423b-db3e-54df-b831-a57602e5ebe9"] [17592186363431 :answer-field/choices "answer-bfd5bc74-b990-585b-b3cb-9771824e5c92"] [17592186363431 :answer-field/choices "answer-6bd62de5-f0c5-582e-9f22-e1174588775b"] [17592186363431 :answer-field/choices "answer-805a0a6c-588a-5652-8708-ef6f81947bad"] [17592186363431 :answer-field/choices "answer-f8e6ef4b-e544-53c2-99a8-afb161df11b4"] [17592186176067 :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Shifted Sphere or Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"] ["question-a7ea971b-d67e-5a60-86aa-35a475db9053" :question/id #uuid "a7ea971b-d67e-5a60-86aa-35a475db9053"] ["question-a7ea971b-d67e-5a60-86aa-35a475db9053" :question/math-academy-id "q-161851"] ["question-a7ea971b-d67e-5a60-86aa-35a475db9053" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d829e8f51e5cd49bf20587f0cd70abd29a8dd1a336a6bad827541726c9cd4416.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4x,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as"] ["question-a7ea971b-d67e-5a60-86aa-35a475db9053" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/df/dfc7359ad44caa959459676a1e54bf1575e3c7e0f771e92a74817d9d1f9cb581.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"] ["question-a7ea971b-d67e-5a60-86aa-35a475db9053" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-a25a1262-2d6c-5d10-9f4a-f4ca15577a84" :answer-field/id #uuid "a25a1262-2d6c-5d10-9f4a-f4ca15577a84"] ["answer-3ac36fc6-724d-59ba-bc41-3af29a3a8de8" :answer/id #uuid "3ac36fc6-724d-59ba-bc41-3af29a3a8de8"] ["answer-3ac36fc6-724d-59ba-bc41-3af29a3a8de8" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-3ac36fc6-724d-59ba-bc41-3af29a3a8de8" :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$"] ["answer-c9301d6b-61d0-5484-9019-19d7b668ce0f" :answer/id #uuid "c9301d6b-61d0-5484-9019-19d7b668ce0f"] ["answer-c9301d6b-61d0-5484-9019-19d7b668ce0f" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-c9301d6b-61d0-5484-9019-19d7b668ce0f" :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi \\}\n$$"] ["answer-cb387986-8ebd-5bb9-877c-9abb1c25a852" :answer/id #uuid "cb387986-8ebd-5bb9-877c-9abb1c25a852"] ["answer-cb387986-8ebd-5bb9-877c-9abb1c25a852" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-cb387986-8ebd-5bb9-877c-9abb1c25a852" :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$"] ["answer-ea6d2993-fd8c-52ec-92fb-7d3841d36a03" :answer/id #uuid "ea6d2993-fd8c-52ec-92fb-7d3841d36a03"] ["answer-ea6d2993-fd8c-52ec-92fb-7d3841d36a03" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-ea6d2993-fd8c